INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('54b20196-fb6e-52fa-8532-3ea710835379', 'USA FRB Supervisory Letter SR 23-9 – Principles', 'FRB Supervisory Letter SR 23-9 – Principles for Climate-Related Financial Risk Management for Large Financial Institutions', 'Issued on October 24, 2023, the Federal Reserve Board (FRB) Supervisory Letter SR 23-9 provides interagency guidance alongside the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) and Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC). The guidance sets high-level principles for managing climate-related financial risks in large financial institutions, ensuring safety, soundness, and financial stability.

Key provisions include:

Applicability & Scope:
Applies to U.S. financial institutions with over $100 billion in total consolidated assets.
Covers bank holding companies, savings and loan holding companies, foreign banking organizations, and systemically important financial institutions (SIFIs).
Climate-Related Financial Risks:
Physical risks: Harm from climate-driven events (e.g., hurricanes, wildfires, floods, heatwaves).
Transition risks: Stresses from shifts in policy, technology, and market sentiment toward a low-carbon economy.
Risks propagate through credit, market, liquidity, operational, legal, and compliance risks.
Six Key Risk Management Principles:
Governance:
Boards must understand and oversee climate-related financial risks.
Management must implement climate risk policies and accountability measures.
Policies, Procedures, and Limits:
Institutions must integrate climate risk considerations into internal controls.
Strategic Planning:
Climate risks must be incorporated into capital planning, stress testing, and risk appetite frameworks.
Banks must assess the impact of climate risk strategies on underserved communities.
Risk Management:
Financial institutions must develop and apply climate risk assessment models and scenario analyses.
Data, Risk Measurement, and Reporting:
Risk measurement frameworks should use heat maps, dashboards, and exposure analyses.
Scenario Analysis:
Forward-looking assessments should test vulnerabilities under climate risk scenarios.
Integration into Traditional Risk Categories:
Credit Risk: Assess climate-driven defaults, asset devaluation, and sectoral risk exposure.
Liquidity Risk: Analyze how climate risks impact funding and liquidity buffers.
Operational Risk: Address business continuity disruptions from extreme weather events.
Legal & Compliance Risk: Ensure fair lending practices and compliance with climate-related financial regulations.
This framework strengthens financial sector resilience against climate risks and aligns with Financial Stability Oversight Council (FSOC) and Basel Committee recommendations.', 'Issued on October 24, 2023, the Federal Reserve Board (FRB) Supervisory Letter SR 23-9 provides interagency guidance alongside the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) and Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC). The guidance sets high-level principles for managing climate-related financial risks in large financial institutions, ensuring safety, soundness, and financial stability.

